  "text": "Solution 2 is wrong. The ideal (x, y², z) is generated by powers of distinct variables, so it is a complete intersection and its powers are integrally closed. Its Rees algebra is a polynomial ring, so it is normal, and the claimed counterexample fails. Its conclusion N = 2 therefore doesn't follow.\n\nSolution 3 is also unreliable. Its 3-generator case is fine, but the claim that every integrally closed 4-generator height-3 ideal must have an exponent equal to 1 is asserted without proof, and its \"≥ 5 generators\" non-normal claim is vague. Its final answer of 3 rests on that unverified reasoning.\n\nSolution 1 gives 5. I can't check the cited Hoa–Stückrad–Vogel reference from here, so I can't confirm its exact statement. The 5 is plausible, though. A natural non-normal candidate is (x⁴, x³y, xy³, y⁴, x²y²z) with z^c added to reach height 3, which gives 6 generators. That fits the pattern \"normal up to 5 generators, failing at 6.\" I can't verify that the 5-generator case is actually normal, so this choice carries real uncertainty.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
